{"name":"Night Owl","hex":"#5d7b89","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"night owl","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An owl (order Strigiformes) that is nocturnal.","One who goes to bed late, or stays up late at night or in the early hours of the morning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/night_owl"}],"entries":[{"title":"Owl","blocks":[{"text":"Owls are birds from the order Strigiformes, which includes over 200 species of mostly solitary and nocturnal birds of prey typified by an upright stance, a large, broad head, binocular vision, binaural hearing, sharp talons, and feathers adapted for silent flight. Exceptions include the diurnal northern hawk-owl and the gregarious burrowing owl."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Bubo_bubo_sibiricus_-_01.JPG/330px-Bubo_bubo_sibiricus_-_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Owl","wikidata":"Q25222"},{"title":"Night owl","blocks":[{"text":"A night owl, evening person, or simply owl, is a person who tends or prefers to be active late at night and into the early morning, and to sleep and wake up later than is considered normal; night owls often work or engage in recreational activities late into the night, and sleep until relatively late in the day. People with delayed sleep phase syndrome are often described as night owls."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/A_Young_Man_Reading_by_Candlelight_%28Matthias_Stom%29_-_Nationalmuseum_-_23887.tif/lossy-page1-330px-A_Young_Man_Reading_by_Candlelight_%28Matthias_Stom%29_-_Nationalmuseum_-_23887.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Night_owl","wikidata":"Q3272661"},{"title":"Night","blocks":[{"text":"Night, or nighttime, is the period of darkness when the Sun is below the horizon. Daylight illuminates one side of Earth, leaving the other in darkness. The opposite of nighttime is daytime. Earth's rotation causes the appearance of sunrise and sunset. Moonlight, airglow, starlight, and light pollution dimly illuminate night. The duration of day, night, and twilight varies depending on the time of year and the observer's latitude."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Savault_Chapel_Under_Milky_Way_BLS.jpg/330px-Savault_Chapel_Under_Milky_Way_BLS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Night","wikidata":"Q575"},{"title":"Midnight blue","blocks":[{"text":"Midnight blue is a dark shade of blue named for its resemblance to the apparently blue color of a moonlit night sky around a full moon. Midnight blue is identifiably blue to the eye in sunlight or full-spectrum light, but can appear black under certain more limited spectra sometimes found in artificial lighting. It is similar to navy, which is also a dark blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/D%C3%BCsseldorf_Midnight_view.jpg/330px-D%C3%BCsseldorf_Midnight_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midnight_blue","wikidata":"Q947638"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Owl_range.png/500px-Owl_range.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Owl_range.png/1280px-Owl_range.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Owl_range.png/500px-Owl_range.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOwl_range.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Chouettes.jpg/500px-Chouettes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Chouettes.jpg/1280px-Chouettes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Chouettes.jpg/500px-Chouettes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tawny owl (Strix aluco), left, and western barn owl (Tyto alba), right, belonging respectively to the Strigidae and Tytonidae families","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChouettes.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Athene_cuniculariaa.jpg/500px-Athene_cuniculariaa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Athene_cuniculariaa.jpg/500px-Athene_cuniculariaa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Burrowing owl (Athene cunicularia)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAthene_cuniculariaa.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Owl._Birds_-_defenders_of_the_forest._USSR_stamp._1979.jpg/500px-Owl._Birds_-_defenders_of_the_forest._USSR_stamp._1979.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Owl._Birds_-_defenders_of_the_forest._USSR_stamp._1979.jpg/1280px-Owl._Birds_-_defenders_of_the_forest._USSR_stamp._1979.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Owl._Birds_-_defenders_of_the_forest._USSR_stamp._1979.jpg/500px-Owl._Birds_-_defenders_of_the_forest._USSR_stamp._1979.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A stamp from the Soviet Union, 1979","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOwl._Birds_-_defenders_of_the_fores